"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,610\nThe Learning Brain. Series 8: Assessment That Teaches\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,333\nEpisode 2\n\n3\n00:00:07,413 --> 00:00:09,835\nQuizzing in real classes\n\n4\n00:00:12,635 --> 00:00:14,376\nLab studies are one thing.\n\n5\n00:00:14,723 --> 00:00:16,638\nReal courses have real grades,\n\n6\n00:00:16,658 --> 00:00:18,980\nbusy students, and messy schedules.\n\n7\n00:00:19,314 --> 00:00:20,905\nDoes quizzing still help there?\n\n8\n00:00:21,075 --> 00:00:24,282\nIn 2021, Chunliang Yang,\n\n9\n00:00:24,527 --> 00:00:29,136\nDavid Shanks and colleagues gathered two hundred twenty two classroom studies,\n\n10\n00:00:29,445 --> 00:00:32,035\nwith more than forty eight thousand students,\n\n11\n00:00:32,232 --> 00:00:34,579\nfrom primary school to university.\n\n12\n00:00:34,749 --> 00:00:39,048\nClass quizzes raised achievement by about half a standard deviation,\n\n13\n00:00:39,328 --> 00:00:42,112\nan effect size of zero point five.\n\n14\n00:00:42,683 --> 00:00:46,124\nThat is large by the standards of education research.\n\n15\n00:00:46,432 --> 00:00:49,088\nAnd it held across school levels and subjects.\n\n16\n00:00:49,258 --> 00:00:51,672\nThe benefit was not limited to facts.\n\n17\n00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:56,397\nQuizzing also helped with understanding concepts and solving problems,\n\n18\n00:00:56,642 --> 00:00:59,757\nwhich counters the worry that testing is just drill.\n\n19\n00:01:00,344 --> 00:01:03,319\nWell designed quizzes can build understanding,\n\n20\n00:01:03,548 --> 00:01:05,071\nnot just memorization.\n\n21\n00:01:05,241 --> 00:01:07,443\nSeveral details help in practice.\n\n22\n00:01:07,769 --> 00:01:13,272\nThe format mattered less than expected: multiple choice and short answer both worked.\n\n23\n00:01:13,689 --> 00:01:16,568\nQuizzes that were graded and ungraded both helped.\n\n24\n00:01:16,884 --> 00:01:21,064\nAnd repeated quizzing across days helped more than a single quiz.\n\n25\n00:01:21,459 --> 00:01:25,360\nWhether students answered alone or in groups made little difference.\n\n26\n00:01:25,530 --> 00:01:30,099\nThe authors point to three reasons: extra exposure to the material,\n\n27\n00:01:30,193 --> 00:01:33,028\npractice in the form the final test will take,\n\n28\n00:01:33,273 --> 00:01:34,900\nand motivation to keep up.\n\n29\n00:01:35,547 --> 00:01:38,621\nQuizzes also show instructors where the class is,\n\n30\n00:01:38,789 --> 00:01:40,239\nin time to adjust.\n\n31\n00:01:40,409 --> 00:01:41,239\nFor teaching.\n\n32\n00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:43,413\nGive short, frequent quizzes,\n\n33\n00:01:43,433 --> 00:01:45,425\nweekly or even every class.\n\n34\n00:01:45,761 --> 00:01:47,236\nKeep them low stakes.\n\n35\n00:01:47,468 --> 00:01:49,735\nRepeat key questions across weeks.\n\n36\n00:01:49,973 --> 00:01:53,808\nAnd let quizzes look like the questions students will face on exams.\n\n37\n00:01:54,187 --> 00:01:57,904\nOne tip to share with students: treat quizzes as practice,\n\n38\n00:01:57,924 --> 00:01:58,942\nnot judgment.\n\n39\n00:01:59,112 --> 00:02:00,641\nSmall quizzes, often.\n\n40\n00:02:00,895 --> 00:02:02,855\nIt adds up to a lot of learning.\n\n"